(; expect dk=1 complete=0 ;)
(; Checked with --eta.

   With eta in the conversion, `c` and `x => y => c x y` are the same term, so `g c` is an
   instance of the left-hand side below modulo eta and rewrites to `c a b`.

   dk_tcalc --eta (complete matching, the default) matches patterns modulo eta: a target that
   is not an abstraction stands for its eta-expansion, and the assertion holds.  dk check --eta
   uses eta in the conversion test only, not in matching: `g c` stays as it is and the
   assertion fails; dk_tcalc --eta --dk-matching does the same.

   Without --eta both refuse this file. ;)

A : Type.
a : A.
b : A.
c : A -> A -> A.
def g : (A -> A -> A) -> A.

[F] g (x => y => F x y) --> F a b.

#ASSERT g c == c a b.
